    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-134163",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a2ac0065-9315-547a-a9cf-c6a5abea1aed",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing Covariance",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx={-2,\\,5,\\,1,\\,4},\\,y={-4,\\,2,\\,4,\\,-6}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ((-2)+5+1+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ((-4)+2+4+(-6)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-2\\,-\\,2)(-4\\,-\\,(-1))+(5\\,-\\,2)(2\\,-\\,(-1))+(1\\,-\\,2)(4\\,-\\,(-1))+(4\\,-\\,2)(-6\\,-\\,(-1))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-4)(-3)+(3)(3)+(-1)(5)+(2)(-5)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[12+9-5-10] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "2"
            },
            {
