    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-134066",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a2ac0065-9315-547a-a9cf-c6a5abea1aed",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing Covariance",
      "problem": "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx={4,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,5},\\,y={6,\\,3,\\,12,\\,11}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=2$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=8.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$",
      "worked_solution": "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-2)(6-8)+(-1-2)(3-8)+(0-2)(12-8)+(5-2)(11-8)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2)(-2)+(-3)(-5)+(-2)(4)+(3)(3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-4+15-8+9] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
