    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-134204",
      "knowledge_point_id": "cee807c1-dd86-5b0e-a595-5c5754c47e4c",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing Covariance in Context",
      "problem": "| **Age, in years ($x$)** | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Weight, in kg ($y$)** | $30$ | $34$ | $38$ | $42$ |\n\nTara plays soccer. The table above shows the ages and corresponding weights of four of Tara's teammates.\nCompute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=12$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=36.$",
      "worked_solution": "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(10\\,-\\,12)(30\\,-\\,36)+(11\\,-\\,12)(34\\,-\\,36)+(12\\,-\\,12)(38\\,-\\,36)+(15\\,-\\,12)(42\\,-\\,36)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-2)(-6)+(-1)(-2)+(0)(2)+(3)(6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[12+2+0+18] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
