      "math_academy_id": "q-134212",
      "knowledge_point_id": "cee807c1-dd86-5b0e-a595-5c5754c47e4c",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing Covariance in Context",
      "problem": "| **Age, in years ($x$)** | $45$ | $39$ | $43$ | $37$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **IQ Score ($y$)** | $90$ | $82$ | $75$ | $85$ |\n\nThe table above shows the ages and corresponding IQ scores of some of Jerry's colleagues. Compute the covariance of the data sets.",
      "worked_solution": "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (45+39+43+37) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 164 \\\\  & =41 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (90+82+75+85) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 332 \\\\  & =83\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(45\\,-\\,41)(90\\,-\\,83)\\,+\\,(39\\,-\\,41)(82\\,-\\,83)\\,+\\,(43\\,-\\,41)(75\\,-\\,83)\\,+\\,(37\\,-\\,41)(85\\,-\\,83)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4)(7)+(-2)(-1)+(2)(-8)+(-4)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[28+2-16-8] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
